When can `npg_end > npg`? Consider: `npg_end` is incremented once per PG line. It's decremented when a PP link removes a previous entry. So `npg_end <= npg` always... 

Hmm, unless the removal logic has a bug. Look at the removal:
```c
if (hrecs->npg_end > 0 &&
    hrecs->pg_end[hrecs->npg_end-1] == p_id) {
    hrecs->npg_end--;
} else {
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < hrecs->npg_end; i++) {
        if (hrecs->pg_end[i] == p_id) {
            memmove(...);
            hrecs->npg_end--;
        }
    }
}
```

This removes at most one entry. So `npg_end` is still `<= npg`.

Wait, but what about the case where a PG line has a PP to a PG that appears LATER (forward reference)? In that case, `k == kh_end` (not found yet), so `prev_id = -1`, and the current PG is added to `npg_end`. Then when the later PG is added, it's also added to `npg_end`. So both are in `npg_end`. That's fine, `npg_end` still `<= npg`.

Hmm, let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is different. Let me look at the actual OSS-Fuzz issue. Let me search for the fix commit in the actual htslib repo.

Actually, let me reconsider the "Only leafs?" block more carefully:

```c
for (i = j = 0; i < hrecs->npg; i++) {
    if (hrecs->pg_end[i] != -1 && chain_size[i] > 0)
        hrecs->pg_end[j++] = hrecs->pg_end[i];
}
/* Only leafs? Choose the last one! */
if (!j && hrecs->npg_end > 0) {
    hrecs->pg_end[0] = hrecs->pg_end[hrecs->npg_end-1];
    j = 1;
}
```

Here `hrecs->npg_end` is the OLD value. The `pg_end` array was realloc'd to `npg` entries. If old `npg_end > npg`, then `pg_end[npg_end-1]` reads out of bounds.

So we need old `npg_end > npg`. When can this happen?

